# Permissions

Apps need different things from the operating system: a dictation app needs the microphone, a screen recorder needs screen capture, a text tool that types into other apps needs accessibility. Oriel gives you one way to handle them on Linux, Windows and macOS:

1. **Declare** what the app needs, once, in `build.zig`.
2. Oriel writes it into each platform's package (the macOS `Info.plist` usage texts and entitlements).
3. **Request and check** at runtime, from Zig or JavaScript.
4. The webview's own requests (`getUserMedia`, geolocation, notifications) are **allowed only for declared permissions**, and only for your app's pages.

## Kinds

| Kind | What it covers |
| --- | --- |
| `microphone` | Recording from a microphone |
| `camera` | The camera |
| `screen_capture` | Recording the screen |
| `accessibility` | Controlling other apps: synthetic input, copy and paste into them |
| `location` | The user's location |
| `notifications` | Showing notifications |
| `system_audio` | Recording the audio other apps play |

## 1. Declare

```sh
oriel permission add microphone "Dictation turns your speech into text"
oriel permission add accessibility
oriel permission list
```

This edits the `addApp` options in `build.zig`:

```zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    .name = "my-app",
    // ...
    .permissions = .{
        .microphone = "Dictation turns your speech into text",
        .accessibility = "", // "" = a default text
    },
});
```

The text is what the OS shows the user when it asks. Modules that need a permission declare it themselves: `audio_capture` adds `microphone` and `system_audio`.

Pass the declaration to the app (the `oriel init` templates already do):

```zig
return oriel.main(init, api, .{
    .id = "com.example.App",
    // ...
    .permissions = app.permissions,
});
```

A permission that is **not declared** is always `denied`, and is never requested. On macOS, using a protected resource without its `Info.plist` key terminates the app, so this also keeps you from shipping that crash.

## 2. Request and check

From JavaScript:

```ts
import { permissions, listen } from "./oriel";

const status = await permissions.query("microphone");  // "granted" | "denied" | "prompt" | "unknown"
if (status !== "granted") {
  const result = await permissions.request("microphone"); // shows the OS prompt where there is one
  if (result === "denied") await permissions.openSettings("microphone");
}

listen("permission-changed", ({ name, status }) => console.log(name, status));
```

From Zig:

```zig
const permissions = oriel.permissions;

switch (permissions.status(.accessibility)) {
    .granted => {},
    .denied => _ = permissions.openSettings(.accessibility),
    .prompt, .unknown => _ = permissions.request(.accessibility), // outcome via onChange
}
permissions.onChange(struct {
    fn changed(kind: permissions.Kind, s: permissions.Status) void {
        std.log.info("{s}: {s}", .{ @tagName(kind), @tagName(s) });
    }
}.changed);
```

`request` returns at once (`prompt` while the OS dialog is open); the outcome arrives through `onChange` and the `permission-changed` event. In JavaScript, `permissions.request()` resolves with that outcome.

## What each OS does

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| microphone, camera | not gated (camera via portal) | Privacy settings (on/off per user) | TCC prompt |
| screen_capture | portal asks on use | not gated | TCC (Screen Recording) |
| accessibility | not gated | not gated | TCC (Accessibility) |
| location | portal asks on use | Privacy settings | Info.plist key + prompt |
| notifications | not gated | not gated | prompt |
| Settings page | none | `ms-settings:` page | System Settings pane |

"Not gated" means the status is `granted`. On macOS, run the app as a bundle (`zig-out/<Name>.app`, which `zig build` creates) so macOS knows which app is asking.

## Webview requests

When a page calls `navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia()`, the Geolocation API or `Notification.requestPermission()`, Oriel allows it only when:

- the app declares the matching permission, and
- the page is the app's own (`app://`, or the dev server), or its origin has a capability allowing the `permissions:webview` command.

Everything else is denied without a prompt, and logged with the permission to add.

## macOS signing

`oriel package` writes `<Name>.entitlements` next to the `.app`, with the hardened-runtime entitlements for the declared permissions (`com.apple.security.device.audio-input`, `...camera`, `...location`). Pass it when signing with a Developer ID:

```sh
codesign --force --options runtime --entitlements "zig-out/package/My App.entitlements" \
  --sign "Developer ID Application: ..." "zig-out/package/My App.app"
```
